<section id="Ax5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">A.5   MIA in three cases</h2>

<div id="Ax5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the <em id="Ax5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data prior</em> is the empirical distribution constructed from the
training set <math id="Ax5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have
<math id="Ax5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{train}}(x_{0})=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{i}\delta(x_{0}-x^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{train}}(x_{0})=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{i}\delta(x_{0}-x^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any test image <math id="Ax5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the UNet predicts</p>
<table id="Ax5.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x,t)\approx\frac{x-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,\mu_{t}(x)}{\sigma_{t}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x,t)\approx\frac{x-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,\mu_{t}(x)}{\sigma_{t}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We are interested in the quantity</p>
<table id="Ax5.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mu_{t}^{\text{finite}}(x)&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{q_{t}(x_{0}\mid x)}[x_{0}]\;=\;\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i}(x,t)\,x^{(i)},\\[6.0pt]
w_{i}(x,t)&amp;=\frac{\exp\!\bigl[-\lVert x-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(i)}\rVert^{2}/(2\sigma_{t}^{2})\bigr]}{\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{N}\exp\!\bigl[-\lVert x-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(j)}\rVert^{2}/(2\sigma_{t}^{2})\bigr]}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>finite</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.560em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.671em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mu_{t}^{\text{finite}}(x)&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{q_{t}(x_{0}\mid x)}[x_{0}]\;=\;\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i}(x,t)\,x^{(i)},\\[6.0pt]
w_{i}(x,t)&amp;=\frac{\exp\!\bigl[-\lVert x-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(i)}\rVert^{2}/(2\sigma_{t}^{2})\bigr]}{\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{N}\exp\!\bigl[-\lVert x-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(j)}\rVert^{2}/(2\sigma_{t}^{2})\bigr]}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Equations equation <a href="#Ax5.E26" title="In A.5 MIA in three cases ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> express the
posterior mean as a weighted average of the training samples, where each
kernel weight <math id="Ax5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the Euclidean distance between the noisy
query <math id="Ax5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and the down-scaled datum <math id="Ax5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ax5.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Case 1 — Training member</h3>

<div id="Ax5.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pick <math id="Ax5.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=x^{(k)}\in\{x^{(i)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=x^{(k)}\in\{x^{(i)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Set <math id="Ax5.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=x^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=x^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> in equation <a href="#Ax5.E26" title="In A.5 MIA in three cases ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>.
Define the squared distances</p>
<table id="Ax5.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ik}(t)\;:=\;\bigl\|x^{(k)}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(i)}\bigr\|^{2},\qquad\Delta_{ik}(t)\;:=\;\frac{d_{ik}(t)-d_{kk}(t)}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ik}(t)\;:=\;\bigl\|x^{(k)}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(i)}\bigr\|^{2},\qquad\Delta_{ik}(t)\;:=\;\frac{d_{ik}(t)-d_{kk}(t)}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax5.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using equation <a href="#Ax5.E26" title="In A.5 MIA in three cases ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> and equation <a href="#Ax5.E27" title="In Case 1 — Training member ‣ A.5 MIA in three cases ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a> we obtain</p>
<table id="Ax5.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Ax5.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax5.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{k}(x^{(k)},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{k}(x^{(k)},t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax5.E28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Bigl[1+\!\!\sum_{i\neq k}\!\exp\!\bigl(-\Delta_{ik}(t)\bigr)\Bigr]^{-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mpadded style="width:0.660em;" width="0.660em"><mo>+</mo></mpadded><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Bigl[1+\!\!\sum_{i\neq k}\!\exp\!\bigl(-\Delta_{ik}(t)\bigr)\Bigr]^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="Ax5.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax5.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{i\neq k}(x^{(k)},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{i\neq k}(x^{(k)},t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax5.E29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\exp\!\bigl(-\Delta_{ik}(t)\bigr)\,w_{k}(x^{(k)},t)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\exp\!\bigl(-\Delta_{ik}(t)\bigr)\,w_{k}(x^{(k)},t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1" class="ltx_subparagraph">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subparagraph">Small-noise limit <math id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</h6>

<div id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}=1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}=1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="Ax5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}=O(\sigma_{t}^{2}),\qquad d_{kk}(t)=\bigl(1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bigr)^{2}\,\lVert x^{(k)}\rVert^{2}=O(\sigma_{t}^{4})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}=O(\sigma_{t}^{2}),\qquad d_{kk}(t)=\bigl(1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\bigr)^{2}\,\lVert x^{(k)}\rVert^{2}=O(\sigma_{t}^{4}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">we have, for <math id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="Ax5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ik}(t)\;\sim\;\frac{\lVert x^{(k)}-x^{(i)}\rVert^{2}}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}\;\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}\;+\infty,\qquad\Delta_{kk}(t)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∼</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo lspace="0.558em">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></munderover><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ik}(t)\;\sim\;\frac{\lVert x^{(k)}-x^{(i)}\rVert^{2}}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}\;\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}\;+\infty,\qquad\Delta_{kk}(t)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Hence</p>
<table id="Ax5.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}(x^{(k)},t)\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}1,\qquad w_{i\neq k}(x^{(k)},t)\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></munderover><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></munderover><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}(x^{(k)},t)\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}1,\qquad w_{i\neq k}(x^{(k)},t)\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This implies</p>
<table id="Ax5.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{\mu_{t}^{\text{finite}}(x^{(k)})\;\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}\;x^{(k)}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>finite</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></munderover><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></menclose><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{\mu_{t}^{\text{finite}}(x^{(k)})\;\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}\;x^{(k)}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, substituting into the estimator equation <a href="#Ax5.E25" title="In A.5 MIA in three cases ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>,</p>
<table id="Ax5.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{(k)},t)\bigr\|_{2}\;\approx\;\Bigl\|\frac{x^{(k)}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(k)}}{\sigma_{t}}\Bigr\|_{2}\;=\;\frac{|1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}|}{\sigma_{t}}\,\lVert x^{(k)}\rVert_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{(k)},t)\bigr\|_{2}\;\approx\;\Bigl\|\frac{x^{(k)}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,x^{(k)}}{\sigma_{t}}\Bigr\|_{2}\;=\;\frac{|1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}|}{\sigma_{t}}\,\lVert x^{(k)}\rVert_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To see the asymptotic form using Taylor expansion around <math id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, note that</p>
<table id="Ax5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}=\sqrt{1-\sigma_{t}^{2}}=1-\tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_{t}^{2}-\tfrac{1}{8}\sigma_{t}^{4}+O(\sigma_{t}^{6})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>6</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}=\sqrt{1-\sigma_{t}^{2}}=1-\tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_{t}^{2}-\tfrac{1}{8}\sigma_{t}^{4}+O(\sigma_{t}^{6}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">so that</p>
<table id="Ax5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}=\tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_{t}^{2}+O(\sigma_{t}^{4})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}=\tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_{t}^{2}+O(\sigma_{t}^{4}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Therefore</p>
<table id="Ax5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sigma_{t}}=\tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_{t}+O(\sigma_{t}^{3})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}{\sigma_{t}}=\tfrac{1}{2}\sigma_{t}+O(\sigma_{t}^{3}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.SPx1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">and hence</p>
<table id="Ax5.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{(k)},t)\bigr\|_{2}\;\sim\;\tfrac{\sigma_{t}}{2}\,\lVert x^{(k)}\rVert_{2}\;\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">∼</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo lspace="0.1389em">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow><mphantom></mphantom></munderover><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{(k)},t)\bigr\|_{2}\;\sim\;\tfrac{\sigma_{t}}{2}\,\lVert x^{(k)}\rVert_{2}\;\xrightarrow[t\to 0]{}0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax5.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Case 2 — Held-out but On-Manifold</h3>

<div id="Ax5.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a test data point <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> which is not in our original dataset (i.e., <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\dagger}\notin\{x^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>∉</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\dagger}\notin\{x^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>), but sampled from the same generating distribution <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the diffusion process assumptions, the local weighted mean <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> in equation <a href="#Ax5.E26" title="In A.5 MIA in three cases ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> has the same algebraic form as a kernel regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nadaraya, 1964</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Watson, 1964</a>)</cite>, using training dataset <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> with (effective) Gaussian bandwidth</p>
<table id="Ax5.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(t)\;:=\;\frac{\sigma_{t}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(t)\;:=\;\frac{\sigma_{t}}{\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The weights <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> in equation <a href="#Ax5.E26" title="In A.5 MIA in three cases ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> are proportional to
<math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\exp\!\bigl(-\|x^{\dagger}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}}x^{(i)}\|_{2}^{2}/(2h(t)^{2})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msqrt><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\!\bigl(-\|x^{\dagger}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}}x^{(i)}\|_{2}^{2}/(2h(t)^{2})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\text{finite}}_{t}(x^{\dagger})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>finite</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\text{finite}}_{t}(x^{\dagger})</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with a Gaussian–kernel local average of “nearby” training points, where “nearby” is on the order of bandwidth <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. The kernel-weighted local mean with radius <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="Ax5.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="Ax5.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax5.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m_{r}(x^{\dagger})\;:=\;\frac{\displaystyle\int_{B_{r}(x^{\dagger})}u\,K_{r}(u-x^{\dagger})\,p(u)\,du}{\displaystyle\int_{B_{r}(x^{\dagger})}K_{r}(u-x^{\dagger})\,p(u)\,du}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m_{r}(x^{\dagger})\;:=\;\frac{\displaystyle\int_{B_{r}(x^{\dagger})}u\,K_{r}(u-x^{\dagger})\,p(u)\,du}{\displaystyle\int_{B_{r}(x^{\dagger})}K_{r}(u-x^{\dagger})\,p(u)\,du},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="Ax5.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="Ax5.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{r}(z)\propto\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\|z\|^{2}}{2r^{2}}\Bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{r}(z)\propto\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\|z\|^{2}}{2r^{2}}\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\asymp h(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\asymp h(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical distribution. This is a normalization of the local mean defined in Eq. <a href="#S3.E15" title="In Case 2 — Held‑out but On‑Manifold: ‣ 3.2 Membership Inference Attack ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> of this paper, and Appendix 6.4.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alain &amp; Bengio (2014)</a></cite>.
By local moment matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alain &amp; Bengio, 2014</a>)</cite>, combining Theorems 2 and 3 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alain &amp; Bengio (2014)</a></cite> (see their Eq. (28)) yields the second-order expansion</p>
<table id="Ax5.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{r}(x^{\dagger})-x^{\dagger}\;=\;\frac{r^{2}}{d+2}\left.\frac{\partial\log p_{t}(x)}{\partial x}\right|_{x^{\dagger}}\;+\;o(r^{3})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{r}(x^{\dagger})-x^{\dagger}\;=\;\frac{r^{2}}{d+2}\left.\frac{\partial\log p_{t}(x)}{\partial x}\right|_{x^{\dagger}}\;+\;o(r^{3}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Gaussian-mollified density at scale <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using equation <a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.1 From Forward Diffusion to Score ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we obtain the proportionality stated in the main text:</p>
<table id="Ax5.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax5.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{m_{r}(x^{\dagger})-x^{\dagger}\approx\frac{r^{2}}{d+2}\left.\frac{\partial\log p_{t}(x)}{\partial x}\right|_{x^{\dagger}}=\frac{r^{2}}{d+2}\left(\frac{x^{\dagger}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\mu^{\text{finite}}_{t}(x^{\dagger})}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}\right)=-\frac{r^{2}}{\sigma_{t}(d+2)}\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{\dagger},t)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>finite</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{m_{r}(x^{\dagger})-x^{\dagger}\approx\frac{r^{2}}{d+2}\left.\frac{\partial\log p_{t}(x)}{\partial x}\right|_{x^{\dagger}}=\frac{r^{2}}{d+2}\left(\frac{x^{\dagger}-\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\mu^{\text{finite}}_{t}(x^{\dagger})}{\sigma_{t}^{2}}\right)=-\frac{r^{2}}{\sigma_{t}(d+2)}\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{\dagger},t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Notably, this equation should <em id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> be correct if <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> is in a low density region of the support of <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{data}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{data}</annotation></semantics></math>, but these points should generally be rarely sampled by definition. Moreover, the <math id="Ax5.SSx2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> estimate of the score will also be extrapolating at those points.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ax5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implication for the attack statistic.</h5>

<div id="Ax5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In in-support regions of the empirical distribution, the local geometry is generally <em id="Ax5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> flat, so
<math id="Ax5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|m_{r}(x^{\dagger})-x^{\dagger}\|&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|m_{r}(x^{\dagger})-x^{\dagger}\|&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Ax5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small. By equation <a href="#Ax5.E38" title="In Case 2 — Held-out but On-Manifold ‣ A.5 MIA in three cases ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>, this implies
<math id="Ax5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{\dagger},t)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{\dagger},t)\|</annotation></semantics></math>
is bounded away from zero (at fixed <math id="Ax5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>), hence
<math id="Ax5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(x^{\dagger},t)=\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{\dagger},t)\|_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(x^{\dagger},t)=\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x^{\dagger},t)\|_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
exceeds the member case (Case 1). This matches the intuition summarized in the main text: on-manifold, held-out queries denoise less precisely than memorized training points, yielding a moderately larger attack statistic without the divergence seen off-manifold.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax5.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Case 3 — Out-of-distribution (far off-manifold)</h3>

<div id="Ax5.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since no training data support is available in out-of-distribution (OOD) regions, the diffusion model lacks information about these areas. Consequently, the learned score field in such regions is necessarily an extrapolation, and the theoretical derivations established under the in-distribution assumption no longer hold.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">A.6 Training details of Adversarial <math id="Ax6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE on CIFAR-10</h2>

<div id="Ax6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train a series of <math id="Ax6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE on the CIFAR-10 dataset using AdamW optimization with a learning rate of <math id="Ax6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and investigate the relationship between membership information leakage and latent space regularization. The model was trained with a batch size of 128 and <math id="Ax6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\{0.0,10^{-7},10^{-6},10^{-5},10^{-4},10^{-3},10^{-2},10^{-1},0.5,1.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\{0.0,10^{-7},10^{-6},10^{-5},10^{-4},10^{-3},10^{-2},10^{-1},0.5,1.0\}</annotation></semantics></math> to balance reconstruction fidelity with latent space regularization.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison, we considered two variants: (i) a standard <math id="Ax6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE trained solely with pixel-level reconstruction and KL divergence losses, and (ii) a <math id="Ax6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE augmented with an adversarial discriminator (adversarial <math id="Ax6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE) applied between input and reconstructed images. In the latter case, a hinge-loss–based PatchGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Isola et al., 2017</a>)</cite> discriminator was introduced partway through training, encouraging the reconstructions to be not only faithful but also visually sharper. Across both setups, training was conducted for sufficient epochs to ensure convergence (120 epochs). The latter LDMs are trained based on these frozen VAE encoders. For phase two LDM training, we deliberately train 2048 epochs to ensure the membership information leakage. For training split, we reuse the member split from previous experiments as shown in Table  <a href="#Ax4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ A.4 From marginal density to explicit Gaussian convolution ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We sample 1000 images from the well-trained LDM checkpoints and calculate the FID to training split.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, in the adversarial setup, optimization proceeds in two steps per iteration. First, we update the VAE with the usual reconstruction+KL objective and, after a warm-up, add a non-saturating adversarial term (hinge form; equivalent to <math id="Ax6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\mathbb{E}[D(\hat{x})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\mathbb{E}[D(\hat{x})]</annotation></semantics></math>) gated to turn on only after a chosen global-step threshold (we set this to 2,000 steps in our runs). The adversarial term is weighted adaptively using a VQGAN-style <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Esser et al., 2021</a>)</cite> gradient-norm ratio on the decoder’s last layer and then scaled by a constant factor of 0.8; before the start step, its weight is zero by design. Second, we update the discriminator with a hinge loss on real images <math id="Ax6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> versus reconstructions <math id="Ax6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math> recomputed in a no-grad branch (to avoid generator gradient leakage), using AdamW with the same learning rate/betas/weight-decay as the VAE optimizer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">A.7 Why Extremely Early and Late <math id="Ax7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!t</annotation></semantics></math> are Problematic:</h2>

<figure id="Ax7.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:47%;"><img src="2509.25003v1/score_and_density.png" id="Ax7.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/179;" width="264" height="179" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Top: density <math id="Ax7.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>; blue dots are training samples. Bottom: estimated score <math id="Ax7.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <span id="Ax7.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">very early</span> <math id="Ax7.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,10]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,10]</annotation></semantics></math>, the inter-mode region is low-density, so the score extrapolates erratically (shaded band).
<span id="Ax7.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimal <math id="Ax7.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\!\in[10,300]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>10,300</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\!\in[10,300]</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> moderate Gaussian convolution enlarges the support and regularizes the estimator—density bridges the modes and the score points coherently toward them, yielding the strongest separation between members and held-out points.
For <span id="Ax7.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">late</span> <math id="Ax7.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[300,1000]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>300</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[300,1000]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Ax7.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> approaches an isotropic Gaussian and the score collapses toward the global mean, diminishing membership signal.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Ax7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, <math id="Ax7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x,t)|_{t=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x,t)|_{t=0}</annotation></semantics></math> is expected to achieve the best performance. Because the noise added to a member at <math id="Ax7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> is expected to be zero (as shown in <span id="Ax7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">case 1</span>) and non-zero for a non-member. However, in region of low data density, score‑matching lacks sufficient evidence to <em id="Ax7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reliably</em> estimate the score function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song &amp; Ermon, 2019</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song &amp; Ermon (2019)</a></cite> argues that training minimizes the expected value of score estimates (here is
<math id="Ax7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{p_{t=0}}\!\bigl[\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x_{t=0},t)-\varepsilon\|^{2}\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{p_{t=0}}\!\bigl[\|\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x_{t=0},t)-\varepsilon\|^{2}\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math>),
which provides <em id="Ax7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inaccurate scores</em> where <math id="Ax7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t=0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t=0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is infinitesimal. To be specific, for the input <math id="Ax7.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl\{x\in\mathcal{R}\mid\{\mathbf{x}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}\cap\mathcal{R}=\varnothing,\quad\mathcal{R}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo>∩</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\{x\in\mathcal{R}\mid\{\mathbf{x}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}\cap\mathcal{R}=\varnothing,\quad\mathcal{R}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Ax7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x}p_{t=0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}p_{t=0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> extrapolates erratically.
Consequently, for <span id="Ax7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">very early timesteps</span>
(<math id="Ax7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\!\ll\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\!\ll\!1</annotation></semantics></math>) the learned field outside the tightly supported
member set can plateau or even shrink, nullifying the privacy signal.
Increasing <math id="Ax7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <em id="Ax7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">extra Gaussian convolution</em>,
expanding the effective support and regularising the score. Figure <a href="#Ax7.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.7 Why Extremely Early and Late 𝑡 are Problematic: ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> plots the average normalised estimator magnitude <math id="Ax7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x,t)||" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x,t)||</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Ax7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,300]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,300</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,300]</annotation></semantics></math> on the <em id="Ax7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">member</em> and <em id="Ax7.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">held-out</em> splits across datasets. Transient fluctuations are confined to the very earliest timesteps (<math id="Ax7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>), and the maximal gap between the curves typically occurs at early—but not initial—timesteps, indicating that moderately early diffusion steps provide the strongest membership signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, for <span id="Ax7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">late steps</span> (<math id="Ax7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\!\approx\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\!\approx\!1</annotation></semantics></math>) the
forward process approaches an isotropic Gaussian (data information gradually diminish); <math id="Ax7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is nearly
homogeneous, so the posterior <math id="Ax7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(x)=q_{t}(x_{0}\sim p_{\text{training}}\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>training</mtext></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(x)=q_{t}(x_{0}\sim p_{\text{training}}\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> collapses to <math id="Ax7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(x)=q_{t}(x_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(x)=q_{t}(x_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which depends on test images and
membership information is lost. Figure <a href="#Ax7.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ A.7 Why Extremely Early and Late 𝑡 are Problematic: ‣ Score-based Membership Inference on Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the phenomenon. The <em id="Ax7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optimal</em> timestep <math id="Ax7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>
is therefore dataset‑specific and also depends on the noise schedule.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ax7.fig3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="Ax7.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:278.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25003v1/benchmark_ranks.svg" id="Ax7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:384/400;" width="384" height="400"></object>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Average ranks (±1<math id="Ax7.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>) of the five benchmark methods
across 15 experiments for four evaluation metrics.
Higher values indicate better AUC, ASR, and TPR@1%FPR;
lower values indicate fewer #Queries</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="Ax7.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:278.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25003v1/norm_magnitude_300.svg" id="Ax7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:384/270;" width="384" height="270"></object>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>The average normalised estimator magnitude <math id="Ax7.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x,t)||" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\hat{\varepsilon}_{\theta}(x,t)||</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Ax7.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,300]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,300</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,300]</annotation></semantics></math> on the <em id="Ax7.F6.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">member</em> and <em id="Ax7.F6.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">held-out</em> splits across datasets</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
</article>
</div>
